<section id="conclusions">
  <h2>6. Conclusions &amp; Limitations</h2>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Key Findings</h3>
    <ol style="margin-left:24px;line-height:2;">
      <li><strong>The exam has undergone one major structural shift</strong> &mdash; Short Questions doubled from 9 to 18 in 2017, then settled at 15 from 2021 onward. MCQs (15) and Long Questions (3) have never changed.</li>
      <li><strong>Three structural eras exist:</strong> Era 1 (2015&ndash;2016, 27 questions), Era 2 (2017&ndash;2019, 36 questions), Era 3 (2021&ndash;2025, 33 questions). The current format has been stable for five consecutive years.</li>
      <li><strong>C Programming and DBMS are the two dominant domains</strong>, with roughly equal representation. MS Access is a consistent but smaller component.</li>
      <li><strong>Operators, loops, and database keys</strong> are the single most-tested concepts, appearing in every year without exception.</li>
      <li><strong>Program output tracing and simple program writing</strong> are the most persistent Short Question themes, appearing in 8 of 10 years.</li>
      <li><strong>Advanced topics are emerging:</strong> Pointers, structures, bitwise operators, ACID properties, ER diagrams, and normalization appeared only in 2022&ndash;2025, suggesting a curriculum evolution that educators must track.</li>
      <li><strong>Some traditional topics are declining:</strong> Mathematical expression conversion to C, acronym full forms, and identifier validation have not appeared since 2019.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
    <ul style="margin-left:24px;line-height:2;">
      <li><strong>Missing year 2020:</strong> No examination data is available for 2020, likely due to COVID-19 disruptions. This creates a gap in the trend line and prevents comparison of 2019 vs. 2020 vs. 2021 structural changes.</li>
      <li><strong>No annual/supplementary distinction:</strong> The dataset does not separate annual and supplementary examination papers. If some years include both, the question pool may be conflated.</li>
      <li><strong>Community-compiled data:</strong> This is not an official BIEK dataset. Minor transcription errors may exist, though the overall structure appears consistent.</li>
      <li><strong>Small sample size:</strong> With only 327 questions across 10 years, fine-grained sub-topic trend analysis has limited statistical power. Individual year anomalies should be interpreted cautiously.</li>
      <li><strong>Long Question sub-part counting:</strong> Some Long Questions contain 2 sub-parts (2025 Q5) while others contain 3. This report counts each Long Question as one unit regardless of sub-part count, matching the dataset structure.</li>
      <li><strong>Question text delimiter:</strong> The <code>$</code> delimiter used for MCQ options and Long Question sub-parts was handled during analysis but may affect automated keyword extraction if not properly preprocessed.</li>
      <li><strong>Scope limited to Paper-II:</strong> This analysis covers only Computer Science Paper-II (theory). Paper-I and practical examination content are not included.</li>
    </ul>
